          "lesson": {
            "prerequisite_note": "Builds on LVM allocation and raw retirement. This unit adds LV availability and supplied open-consumer facts. It teaches the supported activation commands before the full assignment; mounting, boot policy and real kernel behavior remain separate.",
            "concepts": [
              {
                "title": "Plan the two availability changes independently",
                "text": "One pool needs to become available; an unused raw pool needs to become inactive. Neither action returns extents or removes a PV label. Preserve the already-live application and every allocation while changing only the requested availability states."
              },
              {
                "title": "Stored data can exist while its LV is unavailable",
                "text": "An inactive logical volume still has its allocation, filesystem metadata and saved contents. It is not currently available as an LV device for use. Recreating or growing the storage does not fix that availability problem and can damage data. Inspect the current state before choosing a change."
              },
              {
                "title": "Activate the intended group, not every pool",
                "text": "vgchange -a y NAME makes all LVs in that named group available; -a n makes them inactive. --activate y|n and the common -ay/-an forms are supported alternatives. This lab requires one explicit supplied group name. Read the simulator\u2019s ModelActive column in lvs: it reports each LV, so a group may initially contain a mixture."
              },
              {
                "title": "Availability is separate from mounting and boot policy",
                "text": "Making an LV active does not create a filesystem, mount it or configure automatic startup. The file inspection here is a simulated application reader over the supplied filesystem; it is not cat on a raw device and does not model a Linux mount. After activation, it can read the retained sample contents."
              },
              {
                "title": "Keep the supplied consumer facts in view",
                "text": "Some supplied LVs may be marked open by an application. This bounded subset refuses group deactivation when any of its LVs is marked open, before changing anything. Real LVM can have partial effects and requires broader use/mount checks. No consumer discovery, force, process stop or host kernel operation is simulated."
              }
            ],
